onding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {u}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {v}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}\\cdot {v}_{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{u}_{i}{v}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534448 17592186045417 true] [17592186135029 1064 "Recall that for two vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ the **dot product** is defined as the sum of products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {u}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {v}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}\\cdot {v}_{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{u}_{i}{v}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Note that if we interpret [math]\\mathbf{u}[/math] and [math]\\mathbf{v}[/math] as [math]n\\!\\times\\! 1[/math] matrices, with the transpose of [math]\\mathbf{u}[/math] being a [math]1\\!\\times\\! n[/math] matrix, then the dot product is just matrix multiplication:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\t \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} =  \\mathbf{u}^T\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{bmatrix} u_1 & u_2 & \\cdots & u_n \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\\\ \\vdots \\\\ v_n \\end{bmatrix}  \n\n\\end{align*}" 13194139534448 17592186045417 false] [17592186342140 1071 "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a=0$\n- $a\\cdot b>0$\n- $a\\cdot b<0$" 13194139534448 17592186045417 false] [17592186342140 1071 "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $a\\cdot a=0$\n\nII. $a\\cdot b>0$\n\nIII. $a\\cdot b<0$" 13194139534448 17592186045417 true] [17592186421874 1080 "III only" 13194139534448 17592186045417 true] [17592186421874 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534448 17592186045417 true] [17592186421874 1